<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chandannagar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chandernagore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chandannagar, formerly Chandernagore, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooghly-river">Hooghly River</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Established as a French trading post in the 1670s and fortified from 1688, it was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s chief settlements in India. The British captured it in 1757 but later returned it; after a plebiscite in 1949 it was transferred to India in 1950 and merged into West Bengal in 1954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chandannagar.jpg" alt="Chandannagar" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacred_Heart_Church_-_Chandan_Nagar_-_Hooghly_-_2013-05-19_7893-7894.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chandannagar came into being in colonial times, since no medieval Bengali text such as the Chandimangal mentions it. Historians think the French created the town by joining several localities, notably the villages of Gondalpara, Boro and Khalisani. The first Frenchman to hold land there was Du Plessis, who bought 13 arpents at Boro Kishanganj for 401 taka in 1673-74. In 1688 the director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a>, Boureau-Deslandes, paid 40,000 coins to the Mughal subahdar to gain control of the area and build a factory. The name Chandernagor first appears in a letter of 1696.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-dupleix">Joseph François Dupleix</a> became governor in 1730, and more than two thousand brick houses were built during his administration. From his time until 1756 Chandannagar was the main centre of European commerce in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, trading in opium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, rope and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, and its fine cloths were exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The prosperous merchant Indranarayan Chaudhari, who built the Nandadulal Temple in 1740, made a great fortune there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>War between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> began in 1756, and on 23 March 1757 Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> and Admiral Charles Watson bombarded and captured Chandannagar. Its fortifications and many houses were demolished, and its commercial importance passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> downriver. It was restored to France in 1763, retaken by the British in 1794 during the Napoleonic Wars and returned in 1816 with a 3 square mile enclave. It was governed as part of French India from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> until 1950. By 1900 it was a quiet suburb of Calcutta with 25,000 people.</p><p>A municipality was created on 1 August 1880 with Charles Dumaine as first mayor. Citizens could elect a representative to each French parliamentary house. The guillotine was last used in the town on 22 July 1895.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recognised the right of self-determination of its Indian settlements in 1948. A referendum on 19 June 1949 found that 98.5 per cent of Chandannagar's residents wished to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. On 2 May 1950 the French allowed the Indian government to assume de facto control, and on 2 October 1954 the city was integrated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. The inhabitants could keep French nationality.</p><p>The city on the Hooghly retains Indo-French buildings, among them the Strand promenade with Fort d'Orleans, the Sacred Heart Church inaugurated in 1884, a gate of 1937 marking Bastille Day, and a museum founded in 1961. Jagaddhatri Puja, held ten days after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, is held across the city with a large procession.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chandannagar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Further settlements followed at <b>Chandernagore</b> in Bengal, Mahe on the Malabar coast, Karaikal and Yanam.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-india">French India</a></li><li><blockquote>Bengal was the richest province of India, exporting silk, muslin and saltpetre through European companies at Calcutta, Chinsurah and <b>Chandernagore</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nawabate-of-bengal">Nawabate of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
